;; TEST-EXPECT: PASS
;; CHECK-FAIL: "cannot be pinned"
;; CHECK-FAIL-FLAGS: --ir-target=spv
;;
;; Endeavour 173, DECISION: a shuffle on SPIR-V requires a PINNED subgroup size.
;;
;; This is the main portability trap of the whole feature, and it is currently silent.
;; On NVIDIA the warp has been 32 lanes on every architecture ever shipped, so assuming
;; it costs nothing.  On Intel the subgroup size is CHOSEN BY THE DRIVER (8, 16 or 32)
;; unless the kernel pins it -- and a reduction written for 16 lanes that runs on 32
;; does not crash, it returns a wrong answer.
;;
;; Endeavour 156 already emits the SubgroupSize ExecutionMode, but deliberately only
;; when ALL of: a profile names :simd-width, the local-size is compile-time known, total
;; work-items >= that width, and total is a whole multiple of it.  That guard is narrow
;; on purpose -- over a thousand shipped specs have tiny or runtime-derived local sizes
;; and must keep compiling exactly as before.
;;
;; So the rule belongs to the SHUFFLE, not to the guard: a kernel that shuffles and
;; cannot be pinned is an error naming what is missing, never a guess at 32.  Here the
;; local-size is absent, so it is not compile-time known and nothing can be pinned.

(def-type u-vec (vector ulong :address-space :global :align :compact))

(def-kernel k_unpinned_lane_group (&out out)
  (declare #'(&out u-vec => nil)
           (global-size :set-to 32))
  (let ((lane (to-ulong (warp-lane)))
        (v    (+ (* lane 10ul) 7ul))
        (r    (shuffle-xor v 1ul)))
    (when (< lane 4ul)
      (set! (~ out lane) r))))
